  "findings": {
    "root_cause": "Calibration was captured with --tier esp32_ht40_192 (192-subcarrier baselines), but enrollment was run with --tier ht40. BaselineCalibration::deviation() for an Ht40 baseline accepts both 192 and 128 subcarrier frames, and for 128 it uses only the first 128 bins — so 128-subcarrier frames were compared against 192-subcarrier baselines, inflating presence_z for Node2/Node4.",
    "node_empty_presence_z_observed": {
      "node1": 1.4,
      "node2": 5.6,
      "node3": 1.8,
      "node4": 4.4
    },
    "node_amp_std_median": {
      "node1": 3.56,
      "node2": 0.98,
      "node3": 3.11,
      "node4": 1.12
    },
    "note": "Node2/Node4 baselines have much smaller per-subcarrier std (median ~1) than Node1/Node3 (~3-3.6); with AMP_STD_FLOOR=1e-12, small RF shifts become large z. Baseline amplitude also drifted +4..+5 units between calibration and empty enrollment.",
    "next_step": "Re-run multi-node enrollment matching the calibration tier: OPENBLAS_SYSTEM=1 cargo run -p wifi-densepose-cli -- enroll --all-nodes --baseline-dir ./baselines/roomB --output-dir ./enrollments/roomB-test --tier esp32_ht40_192. Then enforce baseline subcarrier count in enroll so a 192 baseline never consumes 128 frames."
  },
